  "modules": [
    {
      "title": "Module 1 \u00b7 Definition",
      "html": "<p>A logarithm answers the question: <i>to what power must the base be raised to get this number?</i></p>$$\\log_b N = x \\iff b^x = N$$<p>Common logarithms use base 10. We write $\\log N$ meaning $\\log_{10} N$.</p><p><b>Laws of logarithms</b></p><ol><li>$\\log(MN)=\\log M+\\log N$</li><li>$\\log(M/N)=\\log M-\\log N$</li><li>$\\log(M^k)=k\\log M$</li><li>$\\log_b b = 1$, $\\log_b 1 = 0$</li></ol><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>$1000000 = 10^{6}$, so $\\log_{10} 1000000 = 6$.</li><li>$4096 = 4^{6}$, so $\\log_{4} 4096 = 6$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\log_{10} 1000000$.<br/><b>A:</b> 6</p><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\log_{4} 4096$.<br/><b>A:</b> 6</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Evaluate $\\log_2 32$.",
          "a": "$32 = 2^5$, so $\\log_2 32 = 5$."
        },
        {
          "q": "Express $\\log 6$ in terms of $\\log 2$ and $\\log 3$.",
          "a": "$\\log 6 = \\log(2\\cdot 3) = \\log 2 + \\log 3$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 2 \u00b7 Characteristic and Mantissa",
      "html": "<p>For $N>1$, $\\log_{10} N$ has two parts:</p><ul><li>The <b>characteristic</b> \u2014 the integer part \u2014 equals (number of digits before the decimal point) $-1$.</li><li>The <b>mantissa</b> \u2014 the decimal part \u2014 is read from the log table.</li></ul><p>Example: $\\log 350$ has characteristic $2$ (three digits), mantissa $0.5441$, so $\\log 350 \\approx 2.5441$.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>$1000000 = 10^{6}$, so $\\log_{10} 1000000 = 6$.</li><li>$4096 = 4^{6}$, so $\\log_{4} 4096 = 6$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\log_{10} 1000000$.<br/><b>A:</b> 6</p><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\log_{4} 4096$.<br/><b>A:</b> 6</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "State the characteristic of $\\log 4560$.",
          "a": "$4560$ has 4 digits, so the characteristic is $4-1 = 3$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 3 \u00b7 Using Log Tables",
      "html": "<p>To compute products, quotients, powers and roots quickly:</p>$$\\log(MN)=\\log M+\\log N,\\quad \\log(M/N)=\\log M-\\log N$$$$\\log(M^k)=k\\log M,\\quad \\log(\\sqrt[n]{M})=\\tfrac{1}{n}\\log M$$<p>Take logs, add/subtract/multiply, then use the antilog table to convert back.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>$1000000 = 10^{6}$, so $\\log_{10} 1000000 = 6$.</li><li>$4096 = 4^{6}$, so $\\log_{4} 4096 = 6$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\log_{10} 1000000$.<br/><b>A:</b> 6</p><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\log_{4} 4096$.<br/><b>A:</b> 6</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>Evaluate $\\log_{10} 1000000$. \u2014 6.</li><li>Evaluate $\\log_{4} 4096$. \u2014 6.</li><li>Evaluate $\\log_{10} 1000$. \u2014 3.</li><li>Evaluate $\\log_{3} 243$. \u2014 5.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Use logs to evaluate $23.4 \\times 5.62$.",
          "a": "$\\log 23.4 \\approx 1.3692$; $\\log 5.62 \\approx 0.7497$. Sum $= 2.1189$. Antilog $= 131.5$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Logarithms of numbers > 1",
      "html": "<p><b>Logarithms of numbers > 1</b> is a key part of Logarithms (SSS 1, Mathematics). Master the meaning, the main points and how it is tested in JAMB and WAEC questions on this topic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>$$\\log_b N = x \\iff b^x = N$$ Common logarithms use base 10.</li><li>Laws of logarithms $\\log(MN)=\\log M+\\log N$ $\\log(M/N)=\\log M-\\log N$ $\\log(M^k)=k\\log M$ $\\log_b b = 1$, $\\log_b 1 = 0$ Key points $1000000 = 10^{6}$, so $\\log_{10} 1000000 = 6$.</li><li>By the product law of logarithms: $\\log(MN)=\\log M + \\log N$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Which is equal to $\\log(MN)$?<br/><b>A:</b> \\log M + \\log N \u2014 By the product law of logarithms: $\\log(MN)=\\log M + \\log N$.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Which is equal to $\\log(MN)$?",
          "a": "\\log M + \\log N \u2014 By the product law of logarithms: $\\log(MN)=\\log M + \\log N$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Revision and exam focus",
      "html": "<p>Revise the whole topic by working through its key points in order, then test yourself with the 50-question quiz for this topic. Pay attention to definitions, differences between similar terms, and questions that ask you to apply the topic rather than only recall it.</p><h4>Worked examples</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\log_{10} 1000000$.<br/><b>A:</b> 6 \u2014 $1000000 = 10^{6}$, so $\\log_{10} 1000000 = 6$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\log_{4} 4096$.<br/><b>A:</b> 6 \u2014 $4096 = 4^{6}$, so $\\log_{4} 4096 = 6$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\log_{10} 1000$.<br/><b>A:</b> 3 \u2014 $1000 = 10^{3}$, so $\\log_{10} 1000 = 3$.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Evaluate $\\log_{10} 1000000$.",
          "a": "6"
        },
        {
          "q": "Evaluate $\\log_{4} 4096$.",
          "a": "6"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
